<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pandacan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pandacan is a district of the City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, on the south bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pasig-river">Pasig River</a>. Originally a riverside settlement within the lands of the pre-colonial polity of Namayan, it developed as a separate town under Spanish rule and was incorporated into Manila in 1901. In the 20th century it became known for its large oil depots.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pandacan.jpg" alt="Pandacan" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Patrickroque01 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pandacan_skyline_(Manila;_12-02-2023).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Pandacan is bounded to the north and northeast by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pasig-river">Pasig River</a>, to the west and south by the Estero de Pandacan and to the southeast by the district of Santa Ana. Its name is a corruption of an older form, Pandanan, from the pandan tree that once grew there in numbers. In the 19th century its many canals and estuaries leading to the Pasig earned it the name &quot;Little <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>&quot;, and the poet Francisco Balagtas wrote of people rowing through them in the late afternoon.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The community was officially founded in 1574, when Franciscan priests set up the first mission there. Part of the parish of Sampaloc at first, it became a parish of its own in 1712, and its first stone church, begun in 1732 by Father Francisco del Rosario and finished in 1760, was twice destroyed by earthquakes. Its farmers sold small amounts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> to the Spanish enclave of Intramuros, and it also made bricks, tiles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> laces, shoes and small boats.</p><p>In the 19th century Pandacan was home to Francisco Baltazar, known as Balagtas, author of Florante at Laura, the musician Ladislao Bonus, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagalog-language">Tagalog language</a> theorist Lope K. Santos and the playwrights Miguel Masilungan and Pantaleon Lopez. In 1882 the Compañia General de Tabacos de Filipinas opened a modern cigar plant there, and under American rule the colonial government made the district <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a>'s industrial centre, with oil companies building storage depots along the river. A housing estate for the depot workers, the Kapitan Tikong subdivision, named its streets after the characters of Florante at Laura.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Pandacan is a district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> with a population of 84,769 in 2020; its first residents were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagalog-language">Tagalog</a> migrants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulacan">Bulacan</a>, joined by people from other regions after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. During his second term, Mayor Lito Atienza sought to have the oil depots removed; a compromise phased out the large tanks beside homes and created the Pandacan Linear Park along the river. The Buling Buling dance festival is held each January before the feast of the Santo Niño.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pandacan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its lands became the districts of Santa Ana, <b>Pandacan</b> and others of modern Manila.</blockquote><a href="/state/namayan">Namayan</a></li></ul></section>
